            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Case: 5% CTR, 0% Conversion Rate, $2,000 Budget Wasted</h2>
<p>A 3C accessory DTC store encountered a puzzling phenomenon: one product video creative achieved a CTR of 5.8%, significantly above the account average of 1.8%, but generated nearly zero GMV over two weeks.</p>
<p>The team initially suspected data delay. By the third week with still no orders, they began checking the product page—loading speed normal, checkout flow smooth, pricing competitive. So what was the problem?</p>
<hr />
<h2>"Runnable" vs. "Sellable": Two Completely Different Metrics</h2>
<p>In ad creative reviews, many advertisers fall into a thinking trap: treating high CTR as proof of creative success. But "runnable" (high impressions, high clicks) and "sellable" (driving actual conversions) are entirely different capabilities.</p>
<p>"Runnable" measures the creative's <strong>attractiveness</strong>—how many people were drawn in by the visuals, copy, or video hook and clicked.</p>
<p>"Sellable" measures the creative's <strong>persuasiveness</strong>—how many clickers actually made a purchase.</p>
<p>A massive gap exists between these two: a creative boosted by clickbait headlines or sensational visuals can "run beautifully" but leaves visitors who don't recognize themselves as the target audience bouncing immediately. Data-wise, this manifests as the scissors difference of high CTR but low conversion rate.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Three-Step Diagnosis: Is Your Creative "Runnable" or "Sellable"?</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Check CTR and CVR Combinations</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Scenario</th>
<th>CTR</th>
<th>CVR</th>
<th>Diagnosis</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>High click, low conversion</td>
<td>&gt;3%</td>
<td>&lt;0.5%</td>
<td>⚠️ Audience mismatch or unclear purchase reason</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Normal click, normal conversion</td>
<td>1.5%-3%</td>
<td>1%-3%</td>
<td>✅ Healthy creative</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Low click, high conversion</td>
<td>&lt;1.5%</td>
<td>&gt;2%</td>
<td>🔧 Creative not eye-catching enough, but audience is precise</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Back to the opening case: CTR 5.8%, CVR 0%. This "high paraplegia" data combination points to <strong>audience mismatch</strong> at over 80% probability.</p>
<h3>Step 2: Examine Traffic Sources</h3>
<p>In Meta Ads Manager, check the "Placement and Device" report:</p>
<ul>
<li>Is <strong>Audience Network</strong> traffic disproportionately high (this placement typically shows inflated CTR but terrible conversions)?</li>
<li>Among Reels/Stories clicks, what's the ratio of new vs. returning visitors?</li>
</ul>
<p>If大量流量来自非核心版位，说明素材被系统投给了泛人群，而不是你的目标客户。</p>
<h3>Step 3: Ask Yourself Three Questions</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>What purchase motivation does this creative address?</strong> — If the answer is vague, the purchase reason isn't clear.</li>
<li><strong>Are the clickers the right people?</strong> — Check whether interest words, audience age and gender distribution match your customer profile.</li>
<li><strong>Why aren't they buying after clicking in?</strong> — Is it price, trust, or users simply don't know they need this product?</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Action Plan: Optimization from "Runnable" to "Sellable"</h2>
<h3>Action 1: Pause Pure "Attention-Grabbing" Creatives, Stop the Bleeding Immediately</h3>
<p>The creative with 5.8% CTR but zero conversions—team decided to pause it. Reasons:</p>
<ul>
<li>Strong entertainment value but weak product selling point; users had no purchase motivation after clicking</li>
<li>Continuing to run it only burns budget and feeds the algorithm incorrect learning signals</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Decision Criteria</strong>: If a creative has run 30,000 impressions, CTR&gt;3%, CVR&lt;0.3%, and landing page issues are ruled out, it can be basically judged as "runnable but unsellable"—recommend pausing.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Action 2: Keep "Runnable" Elements, Restructure Purchase Logic</h3>
<p>The team kept the original creative's visual style and pacing but made two script adjustments:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Add subtitle in first 3 seconds</strong>: Highlight "what problem does this solve"</li>
<li>Original: "This band is waterproof and durable"</li>
<li>Revised: "Worried about your band getting wet during runs? IP68 waterproof, only $299"</li>
<li><strong>Add social proof before final CTA</strong>: "12,000 people already purchased"</li>
</ol>
<h3>Action 3: Narrow Targeting</h3>
<p>Original targeting was "20-45 years old, US users interested in sports"—too broad, causing mixed audience. Revised to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Age: 25-35</li>
<li>Interests: Fitness tracking, Running, Sports accessories</li>
<li>Behaviors: Online shopping activity in past 30 days</li>
<li>Placements: Remove Audience Network, only Facebook Feed and Instagram Feed</li>
</ul>
<h3>Action 4: Results Verification</h3>
<p>After adjustment and relaunch, data comparison:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Before Optimization</th>
<th>After Optimization</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>CTR</td>
<td>5.8%</td>
<td>4.2%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CVR</td>
<td>0%</td>
<td>2.1%</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPA</td>
<td>N/A</td>
<td>$28</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Daily GMV</td>
<td>$0</td>
<td>$1,800</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>CTR decreased slightly, but conversion rate jumped from 0 to 2.1%, CPA returned to healthy range, ROAS climbed from 0 to 2.3—this is what a "sellable" creative looks like.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Review Checklist: 4 Items to Check in Every Creative Review</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>CTR and CVR alignment</strong> — High CTR with low CVR is a danger signal</li>
<li><strong>Traffic source health</strong> — Check placement distribution, rule out false prosperity</li>
<li><strong>Purchase reason clarity</strong> — After viewing the creative, can users articulate "why do I need this"?</li>
<li><strong>Targeting precision</strong> — Is the audience narrow and accurate enough?</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Final Takeaway</h2>
<p>Creative review isn't about whose numbers "look better"—it's about asking: <strong>Did the people who came in ultimately become buyers?</strong> Using the answer to this question as the final decision criteria for keeping or discarding creatives will elevate your Meta advertising efficiency to the next level.</p>
